I cleaned up `util.js` so it's easier to read, but I haven't tested it. The check that compares the old and new versions on the same inputs needed permission to run and didn't get it. My guess was that "this" meant `util.js`, since the only other file is `README.md`, which has just a one-line title.

```js
// Pull the name (n) from each item. Skip null/undefined items,
// and uppercase the name when a is 1.
function proc(items) {
  return items
    .filter((item) => item != null)
    .map((item) => (item.a == 1 ? item.n.toUpperCase() : item.n));
}

// Split a comma-separated string, trim each value, and drop empty values.
function f2(text) {
  return text
    .split(',')
    .map((part) => part.trim())
    .filter((part) => part !== '');
}

module.exports = { proc, f2 };
```

- **Behavior is meant to be unchanged:**
  - **Skipping empty items:** `!= null` catches both null and undefined, so the old separate `undefined` check was redundant.
  - **The `== 1` test:** I kept it loose on purpose, so `"1"` and `true` still count as 1, just like before.
- **Readability:**
  - The hand-written loop and the one-line minified code are now `filter` and `map` with line breaks.
  - Arguments have clearer names: `d` is now `items` and `x` is now `text`.
  - Each function has a short comment explaining what it does.
- **Export names are still `proc` and `f2`,** so code that calls them keeps working. Renaming them to something descriptive like `extractNames` or `splitCsv` would mean updating every caller, so I didn't. I can do that if you want.

To confirm nothing changed, the old-versus-new comparison needs to run: once you allow `node`, I'll run it right away.

